40	736,1580,946,1862	Fig. 40.	An acorn cut through lengthwise: the kernel is a pair of very thick seed-leaves filling the shell, with the very small, short radicle at the bottom.
41	714,626,1164,2244 - 725,1575,955,1905	Fig. 41.	A seedling Oak growing from its acorn: the seed-leaves stay in the shell at the side while a long root goes down and a leafy stem shoots up.
42	1201,1386,1360,1562	Fig. 42.	A pea with its seed-coat taken off after soaking: the thick seed-leaves make a little ball, with the stout radicle on the side facing the eye.
43	1236,845,1660,2246 - 1195,1380,1362,1600	Fig. 43.	A pea plantlet: the seed stays in the soil while the plumule rises as a strong shoot with small scale-like leaves low down and real leaves above, and roots go down from the radicle.
